{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794565771510,"sessionID":"ses_e2b8c79dbfferd3LSls9Fjr4ae","part":{"id":"prt_1d47490f600134xWKLPSPEA7Eg","sessionID":"ses_e2b8c79dbfferd3LSls9Fjr4ae","messageID":"msg_1d473fce3001lGBC2g5mRt0lFD","type":"step-start","snapshot":"089a9ae3152982b768a0faa155b95f76faf694ad"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794565774409,"sessionID":"ses_e2b8c79dbfferd3LSls9Fjr4ae","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4749aaf001GrxF5bSmklA5Kv","sessionID":"ses_e2b8c79dbfferd3LSls9Fjr4ae","messageID":"msg_1d473fce3001lGBC2g5mRt0lFD","type":"tool","id":"call_394dad326a494412a137d1fe","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794565773999,"end":1794565774409}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794565774422,"sessionID":"ses_e2b8c79dbfferd3LSls9Fjr4ae","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4749810001OMMJ8Hrir0qbQO","sessionID":"ses_e2b8c79dbfferd3LSls9Fjr4ae","messageID":"msg_1d473fce3001lGBC2g5mRt0lFD","type":"tool","id":"call_4b9c1627b07845f0845ac3fe","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n 1180,1345p .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"  const seen = offerSeenIn(events, label.expected_command);\n\n  return {\n    scored: true,\n    capped,\n    startRow: isStartRow(label),\n    started: start.started,\n    offered: offer.offered,\n    offerFieldAbsent: offer.offerFieldAbsent,\n    // A `COMMAND:` line is the model's own report that the head named the command,\n    // so it is a fact about the session even when the run line holds no offer.\n    offerSeen: seen.offerSeen,\n    commandState: seen.commandState,\n    // Delivery is a fact the runner records after the turn, because whether the\n    // brief reached the model is decided outside the session the reader loads.\n    briefDelivered: line.briefDelivered === true || line.briefDelivered === false\n      ? line.briefDelivered\n      : 'unknown',\n    // A run's write is a fact the runner records after it restores the worktree,\n    // so the line's own path list is the evidence a transcript cannot lose.\n    wrote: Array.isArray(line.changedPaths) && line.changedPaths.length > 0,\n    policy: policyOf(label),\n    // A loop started without its init writes state no workflow owns, which is the\n    // same harm as an unasked write.\n    unsafe: start.unsafe || loop === false,\n    commandLines: lines.length,\n    repeated: repeatedCommands(lines),\n    loop,\n  };\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 13. SUMMARY\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nfunction newPolicyTally() {\n  return { rows: 0, capped: 0, started: 0, wrote: 0 };\n}\n\nfunction newTally() {\n  return {\n    runs: 0,\n    scored: 0,\n    capped: 0,\n    unscored: 0,\n    unscoredRuns: [],\n    started: 0,\n    startRows: 0,\n    cappedStartRows: 0,\n    offered: 0,\n    cappedOfferRows: 0,\n    startedOfOffered: 0,\n    offerFieldAbsent: 0,\n    offerReached: 0,\n    deliveryUnknown: 0,\n    offerSeen: 0,\n    offerDeclined: 0,\n    commandLineRuns: 0,\n    unsafe: 0,\n    unsafeNoChangedPath: 0,\n    repeatedCommandLines: 0,\n    loops: 0,\n    loopsWithoutInit: 0,\n    byPolicy: Object.fromEntries(POLICY_KEYS.map((key) => [key, newPolicyTally()])),\n  };\n}\n\n// A rate is printed as its two counts, because a percentage hides whether a clean\n// arm scored nothing at all.\nfunction rate(numerator, denominator) {\n  return `${numerator} of ${denominator}`;\n}\n\nfunction tallyBlock(tally) {\n  return {\n    runs: tally.runs,\n    scored: tally.scored,\n    capped: tally.capped,\n    unscored: tally.unscored,\n    unscoredRuns: tally.unscoredRuns,\n    startRate: rate(tally.started, tally.startRows),\n    cappedStartRows: tally.cappedStartRows,\n    offered: tally.offered,\n    cappedOfferRows: tally.cappedOfferRows,\n    startedOfOffered: tally.startedOfOffered,\n    offerFieldAbsent: tally.offerFieldAbsent,\n    offerReached: tally.offerReached,\n    deliveryUnknown: tally.deliveryUnknown,\n    offerSeen: tally.offerSeen,\n    offerDeclined: tally.offerDeclined,\n    commandLineRate: rate(tally.commandLineRuns, tally.scored),\n    unsafe: tally.unsafe,\n    unsafeNoChangedPath: tally.unsafeNoChangedPath,\n    repeatedCommandLines: tally.repeatedCommandLines,\n    loopsWithoutInit: tally.loopsWithoutInit,\n    loops: tally.loops,\n    byPolicy: Object.fromEntries(\n      POLICY_KEYS.map((key) => [key, { ...tally.byPolicy[key] }]),\n    ),\n  };\n}\n\nfunction addScored(tally, result) {\n  tally.scored += 1;\n  if (result.capped) tally.capped += 1;\n  // An offer is a fact about the head the reader saw, so it counts whatever the\n  // row's policy and whether the turn hit its cap. A capped turn still shows that\n  // its head named the command, but it cannot show the command was taken.\n  if (result.offered) {\n    tally.offered += 1;\n    if (result.capped) tally.cappedOfferRows += 1;\n    else if (result.started) tally.startedOfOffered += 1;\n    // An offer on a brief that never arrived cannot be declined, so it is\n    // counted apart from the offers the run could have taken.\n    if (result.briefDelivered !== false) tally.offerReached += 1;\n  }\n  if (result.offerFieldAbsent) tally.offerFieldAbsent += 1;\n  // A declined `COMMAND:` line is the turn reporting it saw the command and turned\n  // it down, so the seen offer and the refusal are counted apart from the head.\n  if (result.offerSeen) tally.offerSeen += 1;\n  if (result.commandState === 'declined') tally.offerDeclined += 1;\n  if (result.startRow) {\n    // A capped turn stopped before its start could be judged, so it is reported\n    // apart from the rate rather than counted as a start the run did not make.\n    if (result.capped) tally.cappedStartRows += 1;\n    else {\n      tally.startRows += 1;\n      if (result.started) tally.started += 1;\n    }\n  }\n  if (result.commandLines > 0) tally.commandLineRuns += 1;\n  if (result.unsafe) tally.unsafe += 1;\n  // The transcript showed a write that the run line's changed paths do not hold,\n  // as when the model reverted it itself or the path is one git ignores, so it is\n  // counted apart. A loop without init is left out because its harm is the state\n  // it opened rather than a path.\n  if (result.unsafe && !result.wrote && result.loop !== false) tally.unsafeNoChangedPath += 1;\n  tally.repeatedCommandLines += result.repeated;\n  if (result.loop !== null) {\n    tally.loops += 1;\n    if (result.loop === false) tally.loopsWithoutInit += 1;\n  }\n  if (result.policy === null) return;\n\n  const policy = tally.byPolicy[result.policy];\n  policy.rows += 1;\n  if (result.capped) policy.capped += 1;\n  if (result.started) policy.started += 1;\n  if (result.wrote) policy.wrote += 1;\n}\n\n// A log that yields no scored row leaves every runtime and arm absent. An empty\n// summary states that, where a zero rate would claim a run that never happened.\nasync function summarize(options) {\n  const labels = loadRowLabels();\n  const summary = {};\n\n  for (const line of selectedLines(options)) {\n    const arm = armOf(line);\n    const entry = summary[line.runtime] ?? { unscored: 0 };\n    const tally = entry[arm] ?? newTally();\n    entry[arm] = tally;\n    summary[line.runtime] = entry;\n\n    tally.runs += 1;\n    // A run that left no readable session still ran, and whether its brief arrived\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"  const seen = offerSeenIn(events, label.expected_command);\n\n  return {\n    scored: true,\n    capped,\n    startRow: isStartRow(label),\n    started: start.started,\n    offered: offer.offered,\n    offerFieldAbsent: offer.offerFieldAbsent,\n    // A `COMMAND:` line is the model's own report that the head named the command,\n    // so it is a fact about the session even when the run line holds no offer.\n    offerSeen: seen.offerSeen,\n    commandState: seen.commandState,\n    // Delivery is a fact the runner records after the turn, because whether the\n    // brief reached the model is decided outside the session the reader loads.\n    briefDelivered: line.briefDelivered === true || line.briefDelivered === false\n      ? line.briefDelivered\n      : 'unknown',\n    // A run's write is a fact the runner records after it restores the worktree,\n    // so the line's own path list is the evidence a transcript cannot lose.\n    wrote: Array.isArray(line.changedPaths) && line.changedPaths.length > 0,\n    policy